course: dictionary
lesson: 4569
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Heel is one of them. The words around it tell you which meaning is right. «Herding dogs may nip at running children's heels» means the back of the feet, because children run on their feet, not on their shoes.
explain: Read the other clues. «Sore knees or heels in a growth spurt need rest» tells you that heels are a part of the body that can hurt. Ask yourself which meaning fits, then put your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Heel can also be a verb, an action word. When someone shouts «Heel!» to a dog, they want it to walk close by their side. The setting, a dog on a walk, shows you which meaning is needed.
explain: Some phrases do not mean what their single words mean. In «He knocked two brothers head over heels», the words together describe a tumble with the feet over the head. You learn that from the whole sentence, not from each word alone.
fact: Heel has several meanings: the back of your foot, the back part of a shoe or sock, the lower back part of your palm, and a command to a dog.
fact: «Heels» can mean shoes with high heels.
fact: «Heel!» is a command that tells a dog to walk close by your side.
fact: «Head over heels» describes a tumble with your feet over your head.
fact: A blister on the heel often comes from tight shoes.
exercise: «Sore knees or heels in a growth spurt need rest.» In this sentence, heels means: | The top of your head | *The back part of your foot | A kind of dog | The part of a shoe under the foot
exercise: Dad shouts, «Heel!» to the dog on the walk. What does he want the dog to do? | Run away into the park | Lie down and sleep | *Walk close by his side | Dig a hole
exercise: «She wore high heels to the party.» What are heels in this sentence? | Loud noises | Long, curly hair | *Shoes with high heels | The lower back part of the palm
test: «Tight shoes gave him a blister on his heel.» Where is the blister? | On his knee | *On the back of his foot | On his ear | On his palm
test: «He knocked two brothers head over heels.» What does head over heels describe? | A loud song they sang | *A tumble with their feet over their heads | A present they were given | A long walk home
exam: Which use of heel is a verb, an action word? | «Your heel hurts when you run» | The lower back of your palm | *«Heel!» shouted to the dog | «Heels» on the shoes in the shop
